 C for Loop — Notes (Embedded Systems)

 1. What is a for loop?
for is used to perform a task repeatedly in a program.

 Syntax

for (initialization; condition check; control)
{
    st1;
    st2;
    st3;
    st4;   // body of loop
}


Execution order:
1. Initialization → runs once
2. Condition check → evaluated before every iteration
3. Body of loop → runs if condition is true
4. Control (increment/decrement) → runs after the body, then goes back to step 2

 Example

#include <stdio.h>
int main()
{
    int i;
    for (i = 0; i < 5; i++)   // ++i / i = i+1 / i += 1 all work
        printf("Hello.. \n");
    return 0;
}

Output:

Hello..
Hello..
Hello..
Hello..
Hello..

 Loop with statement before/after

int i = -5;
printf("before loop.. %d \n", i);   // -5
for (i = 0; i <= 2; i++)
    printf("in loop.. %d \n", i);   // 1 2 3   (prints after i++ per statement placement)
printf("after loop.. %d \n", i);    // 3




 2. Dummy Loop (loop without a body)

A for loop followed immediately by a ; has  no body — the semicolon itself is the (empty) body, so the loop just runs and does nothing each iteration. Any statement written on the next line executes **only once, after** the loop ends (it is *not* part of the loop).


{
    int i;
    for (i = 0; i <= 2; i++);      // <-- loop ends here (dummy loop)
    printf("%d \n", i);            // NOT in loop, runs once after loop finishes
}


 Dummy loop vs Normal loop trace

Dummy for loop (loop without body):
| i | condition | result |

| 0 | 0<=2 | T |
| 1 | 1<=2 | T |
| 2 | 2<=2 | T |
| 3 | 3<=2 | F → exits, i = 3 |

Nothing is printed during the loop; only i=3 is printed once, after the loop.

**Normal for loop (loop with body):**
| i | condition | Print |
|---|-----------|-------|
| 0 | 0<=2 | 0 |
| 1 | 1<=2 | 1 |
| 2 | 2<=2 | 2 |
| 3 | 3<=2 (F) | exits |


int i;
for (i = 0; i <= 2; i++)
    printf("%d \n", i);   // 0, 1, 2




 3. Missing parts of a for loop

 a) No initialization

int i = 0;
for ( ; i <= 2; i++)
    printf("%d \n", i);   // 0, 1, 2


 b) No condition
If the condition is left blank, it is true by default → infinite loop.

for (i = 0; ; i++)
    printf("%d \n", i);   // 0, 1, 2, 3, ... (infinite)


 c) No control (increment/decrement)
Since i never changes, the condition stays true forever.

for (i = 0; i <= 2; )
    printf("%d \n", i);   // 0, 0, 0, ... (infinite)


 d) All three missing — infinite loop

for (;;)
    printf("%d \n", i);   // default condition = true → prints garbage/whatever i holds, infinite (no body → does nothing, forever)

>  for(;;); alone (with a trailing semicolon and nothing else) is a genuine infinite dummy loop.

e) Control inside the condition expression

for (i = 0; i < 3; )
    printf("%d \n", ++i);   // ++i in the print statement acts as control
// i++  → prints 0,1,2,3
// ++i  → prints 1,2,3




 4. Signed vs Unsigned int in loops

unsigned int can **never** be negative — it wraps around to a very large positive value when decremented below 0.


unsigned int i;
for (i = 2; i >= 0; i--)
    printf("%u \n", i);
return 0;


**Trace — unsigned int i:
| i | i >= 0 | printed |
|---|--------|---------|
| 2 | T | 2 |
| 1 | T | 1 |
| 0 | T | 0 |
| -1 → wraps to 4294967295 (≈4.9×10⁹ on this note, shown as 4G) | F is never reached the normal way | 4294967295 |
| 4294967294 | T | 4294967294 |
| ... | ... | continues counting down — infinite loop |

Trace — int i (signed):**
| i | i >= 0 | printed |

| 2 | T | 2 |
| 1 | T | 1 |
| 0 | T | 0 |
| -1 | F | loop exits normally |

Key takeaway: Using unsigned int as a down-counting loop variable that is compared with >= 0 causes an infinite loop, because the condition can never become false (unsigned values can't go negative — they wrap around instead). Always use a signed `int` for down-counters that must reach negative/zero exit conditions.



 5. Loops with two control variables


int i, j;
for (i = 0, j = 4; i < j; i++, j--)
    printf("%d %d \n", i, j);


**Trace (i=0, j=4):
| i | j | i<j | printed |

| 0 | 4 | T | 0 4 |
| 1 | 3 | T | 1 3 |
| 2 | 2 | F | — (exits) |

### Dummy version (with trailing ;)

for (i = 0, j = 4; i < j; i++, j--);      // dummy loop — no body
printf("%d %d \n", i, j);                 // runs once after loop: prints 2 2


 Condition that is never true from the start

for (i = 0, j = 4; i < j, i > j; i++, j--)
    printf("%d %d \n", i, j);
// no output — the comma operator evaluates i<j then i>j;
// the loop's actual condition value is the LAST expression (i>j),
// which is false at i=0, j=4, so the loop body never runs.

// If it were the dummy version instead:
for (i = 0, j = 4; i < j, i > j; i++, j--);
printf("%d %d \n", i, j);   // would print 0 4 (i, j unchanged since loop never iterated)




 Quick Reference Table

| Missing part | Effect |

| Initialization | Must be set before the loop separately |
| Condition | Defaults to true → infinite loop |
| Control | Loop variable never updates → infinite loop |
| Body (; right after header) | "Dummy loop" — loop runs but does nothing; next statement runs once, after loop ends |
| All three (for(;;)) | Infinite loop by default |
